# T9AMAIL — Project Handover (v2)

## Goal
Enable email read/reply functionality on an HMD feature phone (2.4" screen)
via Opera Mini Extreme. Opera Extreme strips pages to plain text/basic HTML —
no JS, minimal CSS, single-column only. All mail processing happens
server-side (VPS); the phone only ever fetches simple rendered HTML pages.

## Status since last handover
The View Module chain is now proven working end-to-end, on both `penguin`
(dev) and the VPS (deployment target). The VPS itself is now confirmed
correctly serving over the public internet. A working outbound-send path
has been found and tested (Resend, not SMTP — see below). Several real
bugs were found and fixed. Two known gaps remain open: timezone display,
and proper read-flag sync. Details follow.

## Wider project context
T9AMAIL is one module of a broader **T9A** project (an experiment in
running useful services on the HMD feature phone to ease the practical
gap from an iPhone). A second module, **T9AMAPS** (directions/geocoding,
formerly `gmap`), already exists as working code but has not yet been
migrated into the new `T9A/` structure — folder created (`/root/T9A/maps`)
but not yet populated. Local (`penguin`) copies of both are being treated
as the working/dev copies; the VPS copies are the deployment target.

## VPS deployment — now working
### Architecture (as actually provisioned)
- VPS provider: Tierhive (low-cost tier). Domain: **omegasemantic.org**,
  web-facing subdomain **map.omegasemantic.org**.
- Tierhive fronts all IPv4 traffic through their own regional HAProxy
  (Sydney, AU) — there is no user-facing haproxy config on the VPS
  itself, and none is needed. The VPS has **no separate personal user
  account** — all work is done as root directly (this was confirmed,
  not assumed: `/home` contains nothing else).
- Given root-only operation is unavoidable in practice, and the user has
  explicitly deprioritised security hardening for this project (accepted
  trade-off, discussed and confirmed), the app now also **runs its
  service as root** — no dedicated low-privilege user. (A `t9amail` user
  was created and then deliberately removed once this was decided; see
  "Reversed decisions" below.)
- Bottle binds directly to `0.0.0.0:80` — no reverse proxy, no iptables
  redirect. This matches the pattern already proven to work by the
  older `gmap` project's `server.py` (also root, also binds 80 directly,
  also explicitly serves `/`).

### Known Tierhive-specific gotchas (worth remembering if this recurs
or if migrating providers)
- **Backend health checks require the app to answer `/`, not just a
  named file.** Bottle only had a `/hello.html` route initially; adding
  a `/` route was the actual fix for a "backend shows Unhealthy, 503
  from outside" problem that consumed significant debugging time.
  Re-saving the backend config in Tierhive's console (Configure
  Backends → re-submit) also appears to force an immediate re-check
  rather than waiting for the next scheduled cycle — useful to know.
- **Outbound mail ports are default-blocked.** Confirmed via direct
  testing (`nc`): port 443 connects instantly; ports 587 and 465 both
  time out to any destination tested, including Posteo *and* Gmail —
  this is a general policy, not Posteo-specific. Tierhive's official
  answer (support ticket #621, and their FAQ): they only whitelist a
  short list of named commercial relays (Amazon SES, Mail Baby,
  Mailgun, Mailjet, Mandrill, Resend, Sendgrid) on port 587 by default;
  anything else needs an explicit whitelist request. A whitelist
  request for Posteo specifically was sent and is still pending as of
  this handover — but is no longer a blocker (see Sending, below).
- Confirmed via `iptables -L` and `ufw status` that nothing on the VPS
  itself is blocking outbound traffic — the restriction is entirely at
  Tierhive's network edge, not local.

