## Sending mail — solved, but not via SMTP
### Why SMTP was abandoned
Tierhive blocks outbound 587/465 by default (see above). Posteo isn't
on their whitelist; neither, it turns out, is Gmail (tested directly,
also times out) — ruling out both a Posteo-direct whitelist wait *and*
a "just relay through Gmail" quick fix. A whitelist request for Posteo
was sent to Tierhive and remains open, but is no longer load-bearing.

### The chosen solution: Resend (HTTPS-based transactional email API)
Resend is on Tierhive's pre-approved list, and — more importantly —
uses a plain HTTPS API call (port 443, already confirmed open) rather
than an SMTP connection at all, sidestepping the port restriction
entirely regardless of which provider Tierhive does or doesn't
whitelist.

Setup completed and **tested working end-to-end**:
- Domain `omegasemantic.org` (the bare root domain, deliberately not
  the `map.` subdomain, to keep sending identity separate from the
  website) verified with Resend via three DNS records added at
  Porkbun: a DKIM TXT record (`resend._domainkey`), an SPF TXT record
  and an MX record (both scoped to `send.omegasemantic.org`, not the
  root — this was specifically checked and confirmed **not** to
  conflict with the domain's existing Porkbun email forwarding, below).
  Resend's own "enable receiving" toggle was deliberately left off — no
  MX/receiving needed at the root domain.
- A pre-existing, previously-forgotten Porkbun email forward was
  discovered during this work: **`hello@omegasemantic.org` already
  forwards to `simon.kearns@posteo.net`, and was confirmed working.**
  This resolved an open design question (what should the `From:`/reply
  address on outgoing relayed mail be) — decision: use
  `hello@omegasemantic.org` as the sender address for anything sent via
  Resend, since replies to it will land in the real Posteo inbox
  automatically, no separate mailbox or "do not reply" disclaimer
  needed.
- API key generated in Resend's dashboard. A `.resendrc` file
  (`RESEND_API_KEY=...`, intended to be sourced by scripts, same
  pattern as `.msmtprc`) was being set up as of this handover — the
  first attempt hit an "invalid key" error from a script that turned
  out to be missing the `source ~/.resendrc` line entirely; root cause
  identified, fix given, not yet confirmed working as the final state.
- **A real test send was completed successfully** via direct `curl`
  (not yet via `.resendrc`/script) — message sent from
  `hello@omegasemantic.org` to `simon.kearns@posteo.net` via Resend's
  API, confirmed to have actually arrived in the real Posteo inbox.
  This proves the full send path end-to-end.

### What replaces the original Compose Module send-plan
The original handover's plan (`msmtp -t < reply.rfc`) is superseded for
the "send from VPS" leg specifically — `msmtp`/`.msmtprc` remain
correctly configured and were confirmed *functionally* correct (config
loads, password/app-password populated, TLS negotiates), the sole
blocker being the network-level port restriction, which Resend's
HTTP-based approach avoids entirely. The RFC 5322 header-construction
logic already planned (`To`, `Subject: Re: ...`, `In-Reply-To`,
`References`) is still needed conceptually, but the final delivery hop
becomes a `curl`/HTTP POST to Resend's API instead of a raw SMTP
connection.

## Two known, real gaps identified this session (not yet fixed)
### 1. Timezone display
`trim_date` (in `mail_helpers.sh`) correctly parses each message's
original `Date:` header offset, but does **not** convert to the user's
own timezone before display — it shows the time in whatever offset the
sender's server used. Confirmed as a real, visible problem: a test
email sent ~30 minutes prior displayed as "13 hours ago," consistent
with an unconverted UTC timestamp being shown to an NZ-based reader.
Planned fix (agreed in principle, not yet implemented): add one
explicit conversion step using Python's `zoneinfo` module
(`Pacific/Auckland`) — deliberately not a hardcoded fixed offset, since
NZ observes daylight saving and a static offset would be wrong roughly
half the year. `zoneinfo` is standard library, no new dependency.

### 2. Read-flag (unread indicator) does not currently persist correctly
The new `(N)` unread indicator in `browser.sh` (added this session,
working correctly as a *display* of whatever `pager.sh` currently
reports) has an unresolved backing-logic gap: nothing currently marks a
message as read anywhere when it's viewed. Two options were discussed:
- **Local-only** (just move the file from `new/` to `cur/`): rejected.
  Maildir's actual spec encodes read-state in *two* places — folder
  location *and* a `:2,S` suffix in the filename itself. A plain folder
  move, missing the filename flag, is very likely invisible to
  `mbsync`'s own change-detection — meaning `mbsync` would probably
  just re-pull the server's still-unread state on next sync and move
  the file straight back to `new/`, silently undoing the fix every
  cycle. (Not yet empirically tested, but assessed as the likely
  outcome based on how Maildir/mbsync's flag parsing works.)
- **Correct Maildir flag** (rename with `:2,S` suffix *and* move to
  `cur/`): the agreed direction. This should be recognised by mbsync's
  bidirectional sync and genuinely propagate the read-state back to
  Posteo's real server — meaning a message read via the phone would
  correctly show as read on the user's normal desktop client too, not
  just within this one pipeline.
  
  This was specifically decided *against* a local-only "separate
  ledger" alternative, because of a concrete, identified failure mode:
  the phone is a fallback device used reactively for fast-moving
  threaded exchanges (e.g. a same-subject back-and-forth like "what
  time dinner? / 8 works? / can't make it") — if phone-reads don't
  propagate to the real server, the user would end up re-reading
  already-handled thread messages from their primary client later,
  defeating the point of having read them on the phone at all. Real
  server-side sync was judged necessary, not optional, given this
  specific usage pattern.

Not yet implemented — likely lands in `browser.sh`'s `[v]iew` handler,
immediately after `generate_raw` succeeds.

## Bugs found and fixed this session
- **`rawmail2md.sh` had a large duplicated block** (identical
  body-extraction/decode/write logic appearing twice in sequence,
  harmless in output but wasteful). Deduplicated; corrected version
  given and confirmed working (`body.md` output verified correct
  against a real message, including quoted-printable decoding).
- **`browser.sh`'s `generate_raw` was non-functional**: `MAILDIRS` was
  commented out (a leftover from an earlier, now-resolved debugging
  detour into a `merged/` directory approach that turned out to be
  unnecessary — the actual original bug was simpler, see below) and
  `TMPFILE` was never defined at all. This meant `[v]iew` and `[r]eply`
  could not work, silently. Fixed: `MAILDIRS` restored to point at the
  real `$HOME/Mail/INBOX/{cur,new}` dirs (matching `pager.sh`);
  `TMPFILE` defined as `"$SCRIPT_DIR/mail.raw"`. Confirmed working:
  `[v]iew` now correctly locates, copies, and renders a real message.
- **The original new/cur read-order mismatch** (headers.txt showing
  unread at top, browser appearing to show them last) was **not** a
  bug in the scripts at all — user confirmed it was their own error
  (not scrolling to the end of the list in `browser.sh`, which
  deliberately starts at the highest array index = most recent). The
  `MAILDIRS=("$HOME/Mail/INBOX/cur" "$HOME/Mail/INBOX/new")` ordering
  in `pager.sh` (cur processed first, new/unread appended last) is
  correct and intentional, given browser.sh starts from the end of the
  array — the two scripts' ordering assumptions are coupled by design.
  Worth a code comment noting this coupling so a future edit to either
  script doesn't break it unknowingly.

## Relative-path cleanup (completed this session)
All View Module scripts on `penguin` now resolve their own location via
the standard `SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"`
idiom and reference sibling files/scripts relative to that, rather than
assuming a fixed install location or a particular working directory at
invocation time. This was done deliberately, now, while the codebase is
still small — the explicit goal being that the whole `T9AMAIL/` (soon
`T9A/mail/`) folder can be copied anywhere (a replacement VPS, a
different path) and just work, with no path edits required.
- `mail_helpers.sh` — no paths, nothing needed.
- `rawmail2md.sh` — already used `$SCRIPT_DIR` correctly; also
  deduplicated (see Bugs, above).
- `pager.sh` — uses `$HOME/Mail/INBOX/...` for mail data; confirmed
  this is *correctly* left as `$HOME`-relative, not `$SCRIPT_DIR`
  relative, since mail data is a per-machine concern, not part of the
  codebase.
- `browser.sh` — `HEADERS`, `TMPFILE`, and the `mail_helpers.sh` source
  line now all use `$SCRIPT_DIR`; `MAILDIRS` correctly stays
  `$HOME`-relative, same reasoning as `pager.sh`.
- `tmail.sh` — rewritten to compute `$SCRIPT_DIR` and call
  `pager.sh`/`browser.sh`/`rawmail2md.sh` via full resolved paths
  rather than bare `./script.sh` (which only worked if invoked from
  inside the directory).
- **Deliberately not made relative:** `.msmtprc`, `.resendrc` — these
  are per-machine credentials, not codebase files, and should not be
  portable/copied along with the scripts.

## Feature added this session: unread indicator
`browser.sh` now parses the `| read` / `| unread` suffix on each `UID:`
line in `headers.txt` (previously parsed but discarded) into a new
`statuses` array, and displays a compact `(N)` next to the UID for any
message whose status is `unread` — e.g. `UID: 80 (N)`. No changes
needed to `pager.sh`, which already emitted this information. Confirmed
working against real inbox data.

